    "updated_at": "2022-11-10T04:24:44Z",
    "body": "> I do see this implemented in mpv.io. There, if you set a specific playback rate, the remaining time is show per the playback rate. However, current time and duration are still the absolute times. A bit of both worlds, where a user can more easily know how much \"wall clock\" time is left, while being able to seek to \"the good part at 3 minutes\".\n\nThat does seem to be a best of both worlds kind of implementation, and it seems similar to the \"3 minute read\" UI I've seen on some long-form text sites. \n\nI've seen native players which allow the user to see the wall-clock time at which the media will end (I.e., \"10:14pm\"), and that would need to take playbackRate into account, but that doesn't seem to make a strong case for a scaled property either, as a page would still need a lot of script to handle stalls and pauses. ",
    "author_association": "NONE",
